{"answer":"Yes—at least in the supplied evaluations, geometry-based/non-Euclidean or curvature-/geometry-motivated optimizers can beat Adam on non-convex problems. Arc Gradient Descent (ArcGD), motivated by a geometrically structured update with phase-aware dynamics, is reported to outperform Adam on a highly non-convex, narrow-curved-valley Rosenbrock benchmark across dimensions from 2D to 1000D and in an extreme 50,000D setting, and it also performs competitively on a non-convex ML task (CIFAR-10 image classification). [1]

Separately, Muon (a geometry/curvature-structured matrix optimizer) is reported to consistently improve over Adam on the optimization of equivariant/geometric architectures, including ModelNet40, with analyses of loss surfaces and Hessian/spectral properties supporting that the optimizer–geometry interaction yields more regular loss behavior. [2] However, one paper argues that strict geometric structure may not be the key driver of performance, suggesting performance can be matched even when the “geometry” is randomized, so geometry alone may not guarantee gains over Adam. [5]

If you mean “geometry-based” specifically, note these papers differ: [1] and [2] present optimizer designs tied to geometric/curvature motivation and report Adam comparisons on challenging tasks, while [5] challenges the premise that precise geometry is the determining factor.","grounded":true}
